1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Navajo saw — stars
scattered by Coyote.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Navajo elders say: sky stories are told
only in winter — from first frost to
first thunder.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Every family and ceremony tells it its
own way. This is one published version.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
From a fawnskin pouch, he set them out.
In the west: the First Slim One — Orion.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Then seven small ones, copied from the
crystals at his temple: Dilyéhé, the
Pleiades.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Last, he flung a fistful of crystal dust
across the sky: the Milky Way.

7
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:05,000
So most stars have no names — a jumble.
Coyote laughed: “But the sky got
prettier!”

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
One crystal was left. Coyote set it far
south: “This is my star.” Many think it
is Canopus.

9
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Black God’s order, Coyote’s chaos: the
Navajo say the world balances both.

10
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Below bright Sirius hides the Coyote
Star: in Navajo country, under 2° high.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Blackfoot: the lost children in the sky.

12
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

